SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"20th CPC National Congress concludes in Beijing, Xi Jinping presides over closing session and delivers important speech","publisher":"gov.cn / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-10-23","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/news/topnews/202210/23/content_WS63551debc6d0a757729e1950.html","claim":"The congress adopted a revised Party Constitution and called on the whole Party, the military, and the Chinese people to rally closely around the Party Central Committee with Xi Jinping at its core.","locator":"Opening report paragraphs and the closing-session summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous official congress text; direct statement of party-state consolidation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 target period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Xi because he presided over the closing session and the text centers his core position.","contrary_evidence":["This is intra-party consolidation, not public democratic contestability."],"lenses":["4B.5","4B.8","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China's Xi clinches third term, packs leadership with loyalists","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-10-23","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/chinas-xi-clinches-third-term-packs-leadership-with-loyalists-2919033","claim":"Xi secured a precedent-breaking third term and installed a top governing body stacked with loyalists.","locator":"Lead paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting on a high-salience party leadership outcome, contemporaneous and widely corroborated.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 target period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Xi as the incumbent who benefited from and shaped the leadership outcome.","contrary_evidence":["This reports a party appointment process, not an open election."],"lenses":["4B.5","4B.8","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Chinese ex-President Hu Jintao escorted out of party congress","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-10-22","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/chinese-ex-president-hu-jintao-escorted-out-of-party-congress-2918972","claim":"Hu Jintao was unexpectedly escorted out of the 20th Party Congress closing ceremony while the congress unanimously advanced Xi's political vision.","locator":"Lead paragraph and event summary","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters contemporaneous coverage of a symbolic power-display event at the party congress.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 target period.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly associated with Xi because the event occurred at his congress and underscored his dominance.","contrary_evidence":["The exact reason for Hu's removal was not publicly explained."],"lenses":["4B.5","4B.8","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Human Rights Reports: Custom Report Excerpts - China","publisher":"United States Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://2021-2025.state.gov/report/custom/f567dad47d/","claim":"The CCP dictated the legislative agenda to the NPC, citizens could not freely choose the officials who governed them, the CCP controlled elections and appointments, and the NPC removed the two-term limit for president and vice president.","locator":"Section 3, Freedom to Participate in the Political Process","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official U.S. country report with explicit political-process findings and ruler-relevant institutional facts.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Describes the 2022 institutional environment and its continuation into Xi's 2022 rule.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Xi's incumbency and the institutional rules that kept him in office.","contrary_evidence":["County-level elections formally exist, but they are not free or competitive."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.8","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Issue 1: June-September 2022","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2022-09","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/report/china-dissent-monitor/2022/issue-1-june-september-2022","claim":"CDM documented 668 dissent events from June to September 2022, and evidence of repression appeared in 25 percent of events; 37 cases concerned COVID-19 restrictions and 37 cases led to some type of concession.","locator":"Highlights bullets","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Well-documented event-monitoring summary, but it is a secondary dataset and not itself a formal legal finding.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers mid-2022 in Xi's target year.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution is partly indirect because many events were handled by local authorities, but the report describes the national political climate under Xi.","contrary_evidence":["Some cases led to concessions, so repression was not total in every event."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Issue 2: October-December 2022","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2023-01","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/report/china-dissent-monitor/2023/issue-2-october-december-2022","claim":"CDM documented 1,080 dissent events since June 2022, found a relative protest dip around the 20th Party Congress that may reflect tightened security or censorship, and recorded direct arrest/detention evidence in 8.5 percent of cases.","locator":"Highlights bullets","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Event-monitoring report with quantification and explicit discussion of repression and concessions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the target year and the October-December period around the Party Congress.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution is partly indirect but still relevant because the report ties the protest dip to the Party Congress and Xi's third term.","contrary_evidence":["The same report records 51 concession cases, so the state sometimes responded short of pure repression."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Clashes in Shanghai as COVID protests flare across China","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-11-27","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/shanghai-protesters-police-jostle-as-anger-over-chinas-covid-curbs-mounts-2952336","claim":"Hundreds of demonstrators and police clashed in Shanghai as protests over strict COVID rules spread to several cities after the Urumqi fire, in an unprecedented wave since Xi Jinping assumed power.","locator":"Lead paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting on a nationally significant protest wave and state response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 target period.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly relevant to Xi because the article explicitly frames the unrest as unprecedented since he assumed power.","contrary_evidence":["This documents protest response rather than formal institutional design."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Chinese authorities seek out COVID protesters","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-11-28","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/coronavirus/china-police-out-in-numbers-to-prevent-more-covid-protests-2953228","claim":"Chinese authorities began inquiries into some protesters, asked at least some to provide written accounts, and kept police out in numbers in Beijing after the weekend demonstrations.","locator":"Lead paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting with direct description of police follow-up actions after protests.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 target period.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Xi because it reflects the national security posture under his rule, though the immediate actions were carried out by police and local officials.","contrary_evidence":["This is a policing response, not a formal national law or court finding."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China set to loosen COVID curbs after week of historic protests","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-12-01","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/china-set-to-loosen-covid-curbs-after-week-of-historic-protests-2955191","claim":"China was set to ease quarantine protocols and reduce mass testing after anger over the world's toughest curbs fueled widespread protests.","locator":"Lead paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting on a policy reversal tied to protest pressure; the causal link is reported through sources, not announced in a public rationale.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly within the target year and immediately after the November 2022 protest wave.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution to Xi is indirect because the report describes a national policy shift rather than a quoted Xi directive.","contrary_evidence":["This shows limited responsiveness, but it does not itself establish durable political freedom."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UN OHCHR Assessment of human rights concerns in the Xinjiang Uyghur Autonomous Region, People's Republic of China","publisher":"Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights","publication_date":"2022-08-31","url":"https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/3985699/files/1352584.pdf","claim":"OHCHR concluded that serious human rights violations were committed in Xinjiang and that the extent of arbitrary and discriminatory detention of Uyghurs and other predominantly Muslim groups may constitute crimes against humanity.","locator":"Executive summary and conclusions","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN human-rights body assessment after years of investigation and state response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 target period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Xi because Xinjiang policy is centrally directed and the report concerns state policy in a region under PRC control.","contrary_evidence":["PRC officials reject the findings and frame the policies as counter-extremism and security measures."],"lenses":["4B.7","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Xi inspects Xinjiang","publisher":"gov.cn / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-07-16","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/news/topnews/202207/16/content_WS62d2237fc6d02e533532e11e.html","claim":"Xi said the overarching goal in Xinjiang was social stability and lasting security, and that Islam in China must be Chinese in orientation.","locator":"Article body","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official transcript-like reporting of Xi's own remarks during a 2022 inspection tour.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 target year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct and personal: these are Xi's own stated objectives for Xinjiang policy.","contrary_evidence":["Official framing does not show public consent or rights protection."],"lenses":["4B.5","4B.7","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Hong Kong: Veteran radio host sentenced for peaceful activism: Giggs Edmund Wan","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-11-04","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/documents/asa17/6181/2022/en/","claim":"Amnesty said Edmund Wan was sentenced for criticizing the government on radio and fundraising for young Hong Kong protesters, and that he was targeted merely for exercising peaceful expression.","locator":"Opening paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct case-level reporting by a major human-rights organization on a specific peaceful-activity prosecution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Xi because Hong Kong's political freedom was sharply constrained under PRC sovereignty and the NSL framework during his rule.","contrary_evidence":["Hong Kong authorities treated the case as lawful sedition and money-laundering enforcement."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"RSF's 2022 World Press Freedom Index: a new era of polarisation","publisher":"Reporters Without Borders","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://rsf.org/en/classement/2022/asia-pacific","claim":"The Asia-Pacific analysis says China continues to extend its information-control model, describing absolute and autocratic control of information, and notes that Hong Kong suffered a steep drop in the 2022 index.","locator":"Asia-Pacific analysis paragraphs 32-36","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Index analysis from a specialized press-freedom NGO with a transparent methodology and consistent country coverage.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the 2022 index year and the same political environment.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Xi because the report directly attributes the information-control model to the PRC regime under his leadership.","contrary_evidence":["RSF is an advocacy-oriented press-freedom organization, though its conclusions are method-driven."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Dating apps and Telegram: How China protesters are defying authorities","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-11-29","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/coronavirus/dating-apps-and-telegram-how-china-protesters-are-defying-authorities-2953337","claim":"Opponents of the anti-COVID measures used dating apps and blocked social-media platforms to evade censors and police during the 2022 protest wave.","locator":"Lead paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting based on contemporaneous observation of protester tactics and state controls.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year and the late-2022 protest wave.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Xi because the censorship and police response were part of the national political environment under his rule.","contrary_evidence":["This describes citizen circumvention efforts rather than a formal state policy memo."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Human Rights Reports: Custom Report Excerpts - China (includes Tibet, Hong Kong, and Macau) - Tibet","publisher":"United States Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://2021-2025.state.gov/report/custom/74758ae8d4/","claim":"The CCP Central Committee oversees Tibet policies, no Tibetan members sat in the Politburo or Standing Committee, and the report lists forced disappearances, torture, arbitrary detention, political prisoners, censorship and website blocking, and severe restrictions on peaceful assembly, religion, movement, and political participation.","locator":"Executive summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official country report with explicit attribution of central control and documented abuses.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Describes conditions in the 2022 reporting period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant because Tibet policy is centrally controlled and attributed to the CCP leadership under Xi.","contrary_evidence":["Authorities justify these measures as security and anti-separatism policy."],"lenses":["4B.7","4B.9","4B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Human Rights Reports: Custom Report Excerpts - China (includes Tibet, Hong Kong, and Macau) - Hong Kong","publisher":"United States Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://2021-2025.state.gov/report/custom/a68d98961c/","claim":"The PRC and SAR governments increasingly encroached on freedom of expression, including coerced closures of Apple Daily and Stand News, and used sedition and National Security Law charges against critics and protesters.","locator":"Section 2, Respect for Civil Liberties, Freedom of Expression","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official country report with specific examples of media and protest repression in Hong Kong.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Describes the 2022 reporting period.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Xi because Hong Kong's political and media environment was governed under PRC sovereignty during his rule.","contrary_evidence":["Authorities framed the measures as lawful enforcement of the National Security Law."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.8","4B.9","4B.10"]}

**Search And Rejection Summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across official CPC congress pages, Reuters/Investing coverage, Freedom House reports, UN OHCHR, Amnesty, RSF, Human Rights Watch, and U.S. State Department report excerpts.

Kept:
- Official CPC congress resolution and Xinhua reporting on Xi's Xinjiang inspection.
- Reuters coverage of the 20th Party Congress, Hu Jintao's removal, and the late-2022 protest wave.
- Freedom House China Dissent Monitor issue 1 and issue 2.
- OHCHR Xinjiang assessment.
- Amnesty on Hong Kong peaceful activism.
- RSF's 2022 Asia-Pacific press-freedom analysis.
- State Department China, Hong Kong, and Tibet report excerpts.

Rejected or deprioritized:
- Duplicate Reuters syndications and mirror pages repeating the same protest or congress story.
- Later 2023-2026 follow-up pages that repeated the same 2022 facts without adding material evidence.
- Current RSF country pages from 2025-2026, because they were not period-fit for 2022.
- 2018/2020 Hong Kong State Department pages, because they were out of period for this dossier.
- Off-topic trafficking or economic pages, because they did not materially bear on political freedom in 2022.
- A blocked `news.un.org` result, which could not be accessed.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct evidence on inner-party deliberation is still thin, so some attribution for censorship and protest handling remains partly indirect.
- There is little target-year evidence on independent courts, legislatures, or oversight bodies strengthening themselves against Xi; the evidence instead shows continued capture and centralization.
- The public record does not cleanly separate Xi's personal orders from local implementation in every protest or censorship episode.
- More search could help on 4B.4 if you want a finer institutional-strengthening assessment, but it is not necessary for a defensible chapter dossier.
